## Key design decisions

### Formation templates do not store players

A formation template stores layout only:

* formation name
* optional formation code
* sport
* owner level
* owner ID
* grid rows
* grid columns
* background/pitch image
* position icons
* position full names
* position short codes
* grid coordinates

A formation template must not store player assignments.

### Lineups store player assignments

A lineup stores:

* team
* optional fixture/game
* selected formation
* assigned players
* bench players if supported
* status such as draft or confirmed

The same formation should be reusable across many lineups.

---

## Role and ownership model

The Formation Builder must support formations owned at these levels:

* Super Admin
* Association Admin
* Club Admin
* Team Coach / Manager

Use the current SportStack role system and naming conventions wherever possible.

Each level can create formations relevant to their scope.

Defaults cascade down:

Super Admin default
↓
Association default
↓
Club default
↓
Team default

Default resolution rule:

1. If the team has a default formation, use it.
2. Otherwise, if the club has a default formation, use it.
3. Otherwise, if the association has a default formation, use it.
4. Otherwise, use the Super Admin default.

Each level should be able to override the inherited default by setting its own default.

In the UI, show whether the current default is inherited or locally set.

Example labels:

* “Default inherited from Super Admin”
* “Default inherited from Club”
* “Team default”

